// A lifted interface class that needs a bridge on *native* too.
//
// `taggingConforms`'s lambda branches evaluate
// `taggingConforms(lambda1.resultType, lambda2.resultType)` as a discarded
// statement rather than conjoining it, so a *nested* lambda's result type is
// ignored. `adaptLambdaValue` checks the top-level result properly, so the
// mismatch has to be one level down — in a function-typed parameter.
//
// Here the lambda passed to `useWrap` has type `(String => Int) => String`
// while the interface's erased `wrap` takes `(String => Any) => String`.
// Top-level results agree (`String`/`String`) and the nested parameter agrees
// (`String`/`String`), so the only disagreement is the nested *result*
// (`Int` untagged vs `Any` tagged) — exactly what the discarded call drops.
//
// So `adaptLambdaValue` reports the two compatible and does not reshape the
// lambda; the class `ElimCapture` lifts keeps `wrap(String => Int): String`.
// `NativeProtocol` compares whole `TagRepr` trees, so it does see the
// mismatch and asks for a bridge — but native runs `InterfaceBridge` before
// `ElimCapture`, when this class does not exist yet.

interface Wrap[T]
  def wrap(g: String => T): String
end

def useWrap(w: Wrap[Int]): String =
  w.wrap(s => 41)

def main: Unit =
  println useWrap(g => "v" + g("k"))
